("p", "The Kestrel range of side-scan sonar systems maps the seabed and finds obstacles in shipping channels, around offshore structures and on survey routes for subsea cables. Kestrel generated £61.3m of revenue in 2025 and is assembled at the Port Lenham factory."),
("p", "The Kestrel 400 is the core model. It has a maximum working range of 400 metres on each side of the towfish and is used mainly by ports and dredging contractors. The company shipped 1,020 Kestrel 400 units in 2025."),
("p", "The Kestrel 400X is the extended-range version, with a maximum working range of 650 metres per side and a pressure rating for depths of up to 2,000 metres. It is aimed at offshore energy and cable survey customers. Shipments of the Kestrel 400X reached 445 units in 2025, despite the firmware recall in March."),
("h2", "Harbourline software"),
("p", "Harbourline is a cloud platform that combines data from Kerrowan instruments, third-party sensors and vessel traffic systems into a single live view of a port or offshore site. Customers pay an annual subscription based on the number of connected assets."),
("p", "Harbourline generated £26.9m of revenue in 2025, of which £22.8m was recurring subscription revenue. The number of port customers using Harbourline rose to 212 at the end of 2025, from 168 a year earlier. Customer retention measured by revenue was 108%, meaning that existing customers increased their spending on the platform by more than was lost through cancellations."),
("p", "The most popular new module in 2025 was Channel Depth Assurance, which compares the latest Kestrel survey with the depth a vessel needs and warns the harbour master when the safety margin becomes too small. Eighty-three ports had subscribed to it by the end of the year."),
